        <body>
            <div xml:id="d811">

                <pb facs="0845" n="795" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg795"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Haselton to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-09-05"><hi rendition="#italic">September 5, 1894</hi></date>.
                        (Received September 17.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 12.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Nos. 58,74, 77,97, 142, 145,
                    150, and 156, of my immediate predecessor, I have the honor to report that
                    George F. Carpenter, in some of said dispatches named, has taken such
                    proceedings that the validity of the decree closing to foreign commerce all the
                    mouths of the Orinoco except the Boca Grande, has been passed upon by the “Alta
                    Corte Federal,” or supreme federal court, and that the validity of the decree
                    has been sustained by the court.</p>
                <p>I transmit herewith the application of Mr. Carpenter and a translation of the
                    same, the report of commissioners appointed by the court and a translation of
                    the same, and the decision of the court with a translation thereof.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Seneca Haselton.</hi></signed></closer>




